---
<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc items={[
        {id: "s1", label: "1. Conceptual Framework Linkage"},
        {id: "s2", label: "2. Scope"},
        {id: "s3", label: "3. The Percentage-of-Completion Method"},
        {id: "s4", label: "4. The Completed-Contract Method"},
        {id: "s5", label: "5. Choosing Between the Two Methods"},
        {id: "s6", label: "6. Anticipated Losses on Construction Contracts"},
        {id: "s7", label: "7. Absorption Into the General Revenue Recognition Model (ASBJ Statement No. 29)"},
        {id: "s8", label: "8. Worked Example — Percentage-of-Completion Using the Cost-to-Cost Method"},
        {id: "s9", label: "9. Worked Example — Recognising an Anticipated Loss"},
        {id: "s10", label: "10. Disclosure Requirements"},
        {id: "s11", label: "11. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates"},
        {id: "s12", label: "12. Relationship to IAS/IFRS — the IAS 11 to IFRS 15 Parallel"},
        {id: "s13", label: "13. Statement Mapping"},
        {id: "s14", label: "14. Related Standards"},
        {id: "s15", label: "15. Glossary"}
    ]} />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="active"
    title="Current Status"
    explanation="This Statement remains formally referenced, but since ASBJ Statement No. 29 (Revenue Recognition) came into effect, construction contract revenue is now GENERALLY analysed under the general over-time/point-in-time revenue recognition model — closely mirroring how IAS 11 (Construction Contracts) was internationally withdrawn and folded into IFRS 15."
    historical="Extended reference poster. Self-contained, no internet connection required."
    link="/japangaap/asbj29"
    linkText="ASBJ Statement No. 29 — Accounting Standard for Revenue Recognition"
  />

  <div class="wrap">

<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Recognising revenue as work progresses, rather than only at completion</div><div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 15 addresses REVENUE and cost recognition for CONSTRUCTION-type contracts — arrangements typically spanning MULTIPLE accounting periods, where the CENTRAL question is WHETHER revenue should be recognised PROGRESSIVELY, as work is performed (the PERCENTAGE-OF-COMPLETION method), or only ONCE the contract is substantially COMPLETE (the COMPLETED-CONTRACT method).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Construction and other long-term contract arrangements</div><div class="principle">This Statement applies to CONSTRUCTION contracts and OTHER long-term contract arrangements (e.g. certain custom manufacturing or software development contracts) SPANNING more than a SINGLE accounting period.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Percentage-of-Completion Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Recognising revenue in proportion to progress, most commonly via the cost-to-cost method</div><div class="text-supporting">Under the PERCENTAGE-OF-COMPLETION method, REVENUE and COSTS are recognised in PROPORTION to the contract's progress toward completion — most commonly measured using the "COST-TO-COST" method (costs incurred TO DATE divided by TOTAL estimated contract costs) — PROVIDED the OUTCOME of the contract can be estimated RELIABLY (total contract revenue, costs, and stage of completion can all be measured with reasonable CONFIDENCE).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Completed-Contract Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Deferring all revenue recognition until substantial completion</div><div class="text-supporting">Where the OUTCOME of a contract CANNOT be reliably estimated, the COMPLETED-CONTRACT method applies instead — DEFERRING all revenue recognition until the contract is SUBSTANTIALLY complete, at which point the ENTIRE contract revenue and related costs are recognised TOGETHER.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Choosing Between the Two Methods</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Reliability of estimation is the deciding factor</div><div class="text-supporting">The CHOICE between the two methods is NOT a free ACCOUNTING POLICY election — it depends DIRECTLY on whether the contract's outcome can be estimated RELIABLY. Where reliable estimation IS possible, the percentage-of-completion method is generally PREFERRED, as it provides a more FAITHFUL, TIMELY representation of the entity's ECONOMIC performance as work progresses.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Anticipated Losses on Construction Contracts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The entire expected loss recognised immediately</div><div class="text-supporting">Where TOTAL estimated contract costs EXCEED total estimated contract revenue (an ANTICIPATED LOSS), the ENTIRE expected loss is recognised IMMEDIATELY, in the period the loss becomes EVIDENT — regardless of the contract's CURRENT stage of completion — reflecting the PRUDENT principle that a KNOWN future loss should not be deferred simply because the underlying WORK has not yet finished.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Absorption Into the General Revenue Recognition Model (ASBJ Statement No. 29)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Construction contracts now analysed under the general over-time/point-in-time framework</div><div class="text-supporting">Since <strong>ASBJ Statement No. 29, Accounting Standard for Revenue Recognition</strong>, came into effect, construction contract revenue is now GENERALLY analysed under the GENERAL revenue recognition model — determining whether a performance obligation is satisfied OVER TIME or at a POINT IN TIME, based on criteria broadly SIMILAR to IFRS 15's own framework — rather than under THIS Statement's separately-named percentage-of-completion/completed-contract terminology. In PRACTICE, the SUBSTANTIVE OUTCOME for most construction contracts is broadly SIMILAR under both frameworks (progressive revenue recognition where the customer receives and consumes benefits as work progresses), but the ANALYTICAL FRAMEWORK and terminology used has shifted to the GENERAL revenue standard, PARALLELING how IAS 11 (Construction Contracts) was INTERNATIONALLY WITHDRAWN and folded into IFRS 15 when THAT standard was introduced.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Percentage-of-Completion Using the Cost-to-Cost Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Recognising revenue in proportion to costs incurred</div>
<div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example: A ¥500,000,000 building construction contract</div><div class="wk-body">A construction company has a contract with total expected revenue of ¥500,000,000 and total estimated costs of ¥400,000,000. By the end of the current period, ¥160,000,000 of costs have been incurred.</div><div class="wk-answer">Percentage of completion = ¥160,000,000 ÷ ¥400,000,000 = <strong>40%</strong>. Revenue recognised for the period = 40% × ¥500,000,000 = <strong>¥200,000,000</strong>, with a MATCHING ¥160,000,000 of costs recognised, resulting in ¥40,000,000 of gross profit recognised for the period.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Recognising an Anticipated Loss</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Immediate recognition of the full expected shortfall</div>
<div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example: Cost overruns turning a profitable contract into a loss</div><div class="wk-body">Continuing the prior example, cost overruns mean total estimated contract costs are REVISED UPWARD to ¥540,000,000, against the SAME ¥500,000,000 total contract revenue — an anticipated LOSS of ¥40,000,000 overall.</div><div class="wk-answer">The company recognises the ENTIRE ¥40,000,000 anticipated loss IMMEDIATELY in the CURRENT period (in addition to any loss already reflected through normal percentage-of-completion accounting to date) — the FULL expected shortfall is NOT deferred across the remaining periods of the contract.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASBJ Statement No. 15 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Method used to recognise construction contract revenue (percentage-of-completion or completed-contract)</span><span>Amount of anticipated losses recognised on construction contracts</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a contract's outcome can be estimated reliably, determining which method applies</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Total estimated contract costs and revenue, and stage of completion at each reporting date</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS — the IAS 11 to IFRS 15 Parallel</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A genuinely parallel international and domestic shift</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-12-12-12"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">J-GAAP</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">International (IFRS)</span></div>
<div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Historical construction-specific standard</span><span>ASBJ Statement No. 15</span><span>IAS 11, Construction Contracts</span></div>
<div class="tr alt cols-12-12-12"><span>Successor general revenue standard</span><span>ASBJ Statement No. 29, Revenue Recognition</span><span>IFRS 15, Revenue from Contracts with Customers</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">Japan's shift AWAY from a construction-specific standard TOWARD a general revenue recognition model directly PARALLELS the SAME shift IFRS made internationally — a genuinely CONVERGED pattern, even though the SPECIFIC standard numbers and formal withdrawal status differ between the two frameworks.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every construction contract event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Percentage-of-completion revenue recognition</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> <span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Now generally analysed under ASBJ Statement No. 29's general model</span></div>
<div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Anticipated loss</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> <span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Recognised immediately in full</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 29</span><span>Revenue Recognition — the general standard now primarily governing construction contract revenue in practice</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Percentage-of-completion method</div><div class="glossary-def">Recognising contract revenue and costs in proportion to progress toward completion, most commonly via the cost-to-cost method.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Completed-contract method</div><div class="glossary-def">Deferring all revenue recognition until a contract is substantially complete, used when the outcome cannot be reliably estimated.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last"><div class="glossary-term">Anticipated loss</div><div class="glossary-def">The full amount by which total estimated contract costs exceed total estimated contract revenue, recognised immediately in the period it becomes evident.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (貸借対照表) <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書) <span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部 <span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書) <span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)</div></div>

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
